  "title": "# multi-account-headless-workers This build is ALREADY registered in the Build orchestrator as `mul…",
  "asked": "# multi-account-headless-workers This build is ALREADY registered in the Build orchestrator as `multi-account-headless-workers` (profile current, 5 steps). Use that record: follow its steps and the model the orchestrator set for each step, tick steps there as you finish them, and record session and cost on it. Goal (Brett): the console signs into more than one Claude subscription at once. The console itself uses one account. The other accounts exist only as headless worker sessions, and the orchestrator sends build jobs to them. 1. Audit existing account-failover, account-switch-handoff and claude-accounts code and reuse it. 2. Console holds multiple signed-in subscriptions: one interactive, others headless-only. 3. Account manager UI: add, sign in and remove worker accounts; usage and limits per account. 4. Orchestrator routes jobs to worker accounts and fails over when one hits its limit. 5. Recording, cost and session booking per worker account. Watch live: Brett can watch the screen you are changing hot-render in the console. Whenever you start work on a screen or element, run `node framework/cli/axf.mjs focus <app> [route] [css-selector] --note \"<what you are changing>\"` (apps: console-web, axhub-web, amberx-droid, walkthrough-studio, demo, framework, console, axhub-android, voicevault). Agent cap: heavy steps (pnpm/npm/vite builds, docker node:22 test runs, Playwright) share a VPS-wide lock. Run each one as `node /opt/axf/deploy/../framework/cli/agent-slots.mjs heavy -- <command>` so only a few run at once. Model switches: a downgrade (e.g. Fable → Opus) is fine. Fable upgrades need no approval right now (Brett, Nov 4 2026: \"If it needs Fable, set it to be Fable\"): a step that needs Fable (schemas, asset types, contracts, engines/kernel, cross-app protocols) is set to Fable and runs on Fable; the switch is recorded on the build. Never launch a step on a higher model than it is pinned to: raise the step first.",
